# November 21, 2026: a second scatter of places across Colorado (region co), claimed by tools/choose.py --thinnest because
# Colorado had the fewest places on the map (19). Fifteen places spread over the state, none of them already on the map:
# the Ashcroft ghost town above Aspen, the Kit Carson County Carousel at Burlington on the eastern plains, Fort Vasquez at
# Platteville, the Holzwarth homestead and the Lulu City ruins in the Kawuneeche Valley of Rocky Mountain National Park,
# the Argo Mill at Idaho Springs, the Central City Opera House, Cliff Palace at Mesa Verde, the Mayflower Mill at
# Silverton, the Baca House in Trinidad, Miramont Castle at Manitou Springs, Rosemount in Pueblo, the Alpine Tunnel station
# on the old South Park line, the Hahns Peak schoolhouse and the Lodore School in Browns Park. Sources were opened in this
# session: Wikipedia as raw text (tools/wiki_raw.py) and, through tools/page.py, the managers' own pages (Aspen Historical
# Society, History Colorado, the National Park Service, the Forest Service, the San Juan County Historical Society, the
# Manitou Springs Historical Society, Rosemount, the Hahns Peak Area Historical Society, the carousel's own site, Central
# City Opera, the Argo's own site) and SAH Archipedia.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
BATCH={'key':'co_eehq','label':'Colorado: a second scatter','added':'2026-11-21 12:19'}
PLACES=[
N('ashcroft-ghost-town-co','Ashcroft ghost town','Aspen','co','town','standing',39.0536,-106.7998,'site','public','walk','','1880–1912',
  'Two prospectors from Leadville found silver in the Castle Creek valley in the spring of 1880, and the camp of Castle Forks City, soon Ashcroft, was laid out in two weeks by a miners\' association of 97 members. A rich strike in the Montezuma and Tam O\'Shanter mines, part-owned by H.A.W. Tabor, kept it going for a few years, but the ore proved shallow. The post office closed in 1912, and the townsite was listed on the National Register in 1975.',
  'The Aspen Historical Society calls it home to several restored and relocated buildings in alpine meadows, with trails, interpretive signs (also in Spanish) and lines of shrubs that mark where other buildings stood. A docent and a small gift shop are there in summer. Interiors not checked.',
  'A silver camp of 1880 laid out in two weeks, standing in a high meadow.',
  'In White River National Forest; the Aspen Historical Society runs the townsite under a Forest Service permit. Its page (read November 2026): 11 miles up Castle Creek Road from the roundabout on Highway 82 at the west entrance to Aspen; $5 for adults, under 19 and active military free, paid at an honor box on the welcome sign; no dogs. Where to park at the townsite was not checked: look for the society\'s lot by the road.',
  [W('Ashcroft, Colorado'),L('Aspen Historical Society: Ashcroft Ghost Town','https://aspenhistory.org/ashcroft-ghost-town/')],brick='?',
  hours='Docent on site 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. in summer; self-guided at other times'),
N('kit-carson-county-carousel','Kit Carson County Carousel','Burlington','co','amuse','standing',39.30911,-102.27071,'exact','open','drive','amusements','1905',
  'Philadelphia Toboggan Company built its sixth carousel in 1905 for Elitch Gardens in Denver, where it ran every summer until 1928. The park then sold it with its band organ to Kit Carson County for $1,200, delivery by train included. It sat in storage for six years in the Depression and has run at the county fairgrounds in Burlington since 1937. It was listed on the National Register in 1978 and made a National Historic Landmark in 1987.',
  'A three-row stationary carousel (the animals do not go up and down) in a 12-sided frame building. Its own site says it is the only antique carousel in America with original paint on both the scenery panels and the animals, and the only surviving menagerie carousel by the company: Wikipedia lists camels, giraffes, zebras, deer, goats, a lion, a tiger and a hippocampus among its figures, and a 1909 Wurlitzer band organ. Four animals stolen in 1981 were found in Kansas and carry markers. It runs.',
  'A 1905 menagerie carousel in its original paint, on the plains by I-70.',
  'Owned by Kit Carson County and housed at the county fairgrounds, 815 15th Street, Burlington. The carousel\'s own site (read November 2026) says it opened for the 2026 season on May 23 and gives directions from I-70; daily hours and fares were not on the pages read.',
  [W('Elitch Gardens Carousel'),L('Kit Carson County Carousel','https://kitcarsoncountycarousel.com')],brick='no',
  hours='Summer season from late May (opened May 23 in 2026); days and times not checked'),
N('fort-vasquez-co','Fort Vasquez','Platteville','co','fort','standing',40.19425,-104.82087,'exact','open','drive','','1835–1842; rebuilt 1930s',
  'Louis Vasquez and Andrew Sublette opened this fur-trading post on the South Platte in 1835, about halfway between Fort Laramie and Bent\'s Fort. History Colorado says it was an adobe square about 100 feet a side with walls two feet thick, built by Mexican workers, with up to two dozen traders living there. They sold it in 1842 and the buyers abandoned it the same year; what was left served as a way station, school and post office and then a ranch. In the 1930s Platteville led a WPA rebuilding as a museum.',
  'The adobe fort you see is the 1930s reconstruction, smaller than the original and laid out differently, on the original site; U.S. 85 splits to pass either side of it. A museum of the fur trade is inside.',
  'A WPA adobe trading post standing in the middle of a highway.',
  'History Colorado looks after the historic site and the Town of Platteville runs the Fort Vasquez Welcome Center (History Colorado page, read November 2026, which gives the hours). OpenStreetMap maps a lot on U.S. 85 about 100 m north.',
  [W('Fort Vasquez'),L('History Colorado: Fort Vasquez','https://historycolorado.org/node/3669')],th=[40.19516,-104.82081],thn='Lot on U.S. 85 (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no',
  hours='Wednesday, Saturday and Sunday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('holzwarth-historic-site','Holzwarth Historic Site (Never Summer Ranch)','Grand Lake','co','hotel','standing',40.3725,-105.86167,'site','public','walk','','1917–1974',
  'John Holzwarth, a Denver saloonkeeper put out of business by Colorado\'s prohibition in 1916, homesteaded here in the Kawuneeche Valley in 1917, two years after the national park was set up. Friends kept staying for free, so the family began charging guests in 1919, and the opening of Fall River Road in 1920 brought more; the Holzwarth Trout Lodge became the Never Summer Ranch, a dude ranch run by John Jr. until 1973. The Nature Conservancy bought it in 1974 and passed it to the National Park Service in 1975. It was listed on the National Register in 1977.',
  'Rustic cabins on the Colorado River, among them the first homestead cabin (the Mama Cabin), the Twin Cabins, a tent cabin and a taxidermy shop; of the east-side buildings only Fleshuts Cabin, near the parking area, is left. NPS: the buildings are closed from late fall to spring, and there is a free self-guided tour on the NPS app. Interiors in summer not checked.',
  'A homestead turned dude ranch when prohibition shut a Denver saloon.',
  'Rocky Mountain National Park (park entrance fee not checked). The NPS page (read November 2026): paved lot off Trail Ridge Road with vault toilets; the other cabins are a level half-mile gravel walk from it; grounds open all day, every day of the year, but the trail is not plowed in winter (snowshoes or skis); no pets on trails. Trail Ridge Road east of the valley closes mid-October to Memorial Day.',
  [W('Holzwarth Historic District'),L('NPS: Holzwarth Historic Site','https://www.nps.gov/romo/planyourvisit/holzwarth-historic-site.htm')],th=[40.37285,-105.85453],thn='Holzwarth Historic Site lot, Trail Ridge Road',brick='?',
  hours='Grounds open 24 hours year-round; historic buildings closed late fall to spring'),
N('lulu-city-co','Lulu City','Grand Lake','co','town','ruins',40.44556,-105.84778,'area','public','hike','','1880–1885',
  'Joe Shipler found silver in the upper Kawuneeche Valley in 1879, and the Middle Park and Grand River Land Improvement Company, backed by Fort Collins men, built Lulu City in 1880, naming it for Benjamin Burnett\'s daughter. By 1881 there were forty cabins and a number of businesses, but the ore was low grade and hauling it cost too much. The town was empty by 1885 except for Shipler, who stayed thirty years. The Park Service bought the land in 1949; it was listed on the National Register in 1977.',
  'Wikipedia: three cabin ruins and remnants of other structures. Not checked on the ground.',
  'Forty cabins in 1881, empty four years later.',
  'Rocky Mountain National Park. NPS (read November 2026): from the Colorado River Trailhead on Trail Ridge Road it is a 3.7-mile trail to the site of Lulu City; Trail Ridge Road is closed east of here from mid-October to Memorial Day. The pin is Wikipedia\'s coordinate and was not confirmed on the aerial.',
  [W('Lulu City, Colorado'),L('NPS: Colorado River Trailhead','https://www.nps.gov/places/romo_corivertrailhead.htm')],th=[40.4011,-105.84883],thn='Colorado River Trailhead',brick='?'),
N('argo-mill-idaho-springs','Argo Gold Mill and Tunnel','Idaho Springs','co','industry','standing',39.74287,-105.50661,'exact','open','drive','','1893–1943',
  'The Argo Tunnel was driven between 1893 and 1910 to drain and serve the gold mines under Virginia Canyon, Russell Gulch, Nevadaville and Central City, and so much ore came out at its Idaho Springs end that a big mill was built at the portal. Wikipedia says the works recovered nearly $100 million in gold. A hydraulic accident in the tunnel closed it in January 1943, and the wartime ban on gold mining kept it shut. Local investors bought it in 1976 to keep an example of a gold-rush mill, and it opened for tours; it was listed on the National Register in 1978.',
  'The mill stands on the hillside above Clear Creek, over 110 feet tall by its own site, with original equipment inside (gravity separators, amalgamation trays, flotation cells) and a look into the tunnel portal on the tour. Some large equipment in the outdoor museum can be seen from the street.',
  'The mill at the mouth of a drainage tunnel that ran under Central City\'s mines.',
  'Privately owned and run as a paid tour attraction at 2517 Riverside Drive (own site argomilltour.com, read November 2026; Wikipedia says tours run all year, weather permitting). Tour times and prices were not on the pages read. A paved lot just west of the mill shows on the aerial; whether it is for visitors was not checked.',
  [W('Argo Gold Mine and Mill'),L('The Argo Mill and Tunnel','http://argomilltour.com/')],th=[39.74261,-105.50719],thn='Lot just west of the mill (aerial)',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('central-city-opera-house','Central City Opera House','Central City','co','civic','standing',39.80113,-105.51364,'exact','open','drive','','1878',
  'Central City\'s people formed an opera house association in 1877 and built the state\'s first opera house the next year, to designs by Denver architect Robert S. Roeschlaub, with much of the labour from Welsh and Cornish miners. It drew noted performers in the late 1800s and was a cinema in the early 1900s. A group of Denver preservationists formed the Central City Opera House Association in 1929, restored it, and reopened it; it was listed on the National Register in 1973.',
  'A Renaissance Revival building of stone and brick with arched windows. Inside, per Wikipedia, a crescent-shaped balcony and a ceiling mural by John C. Massman with a sky medallion and painted mouldings; a creek runs in a flume under the building. Central City Opera owns 28 historic buildings in the town. Interior seen only at performances or on a booked tour.',
  'A miners\' opera house of 1878 that still stages summer opera.',
  'Owned by Central City Opera, on Eureka Street. Its tours page (read November 2026): tours for groups by reservation in advance through its historic properties office; the Gilpin Historical Society also runs tours of the town (gilpinhistory.org). Performance dates are on the company\'s site and were not checked.',
  [W('Central City Opera House'),L('Central City Opera: Tours','https://centralcityopera.org/your-visit/tours/')],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('cliff-palace-mesa-verde','Cliff Palace','Mesa Verde National Park','co','landmark','ruins',37.16667,-108.47278,'site','open','walk','','about 1190–1300',
  'The largest cliff dwelling in North America, built by Ancestral Puebloans in an alcove of Cliff Canyon on Chapin Mesa. The Park Service dates it to between 1190 and 1280 CE and says over 100 people lived there; Wikipedia gives about 1200 to 1300 for its use and says it was abandoned by 1300, the cause still argued over. Richard Wetherill and Charlie Mason came on it in December 1888 while looking for stray cattle.',
  'Stone room blocks, towers and kivas filling the alcove. It can be seen from the overlook on the Cliff Palace Loop; going in is by ranger-guided tour only, with ladders to climb (NPS: about a quarter-mile walk in all, at about 7,000 to 7,500 feet).',
  'The biggest cliff dwelling in North America, under its own rock roof.',
  'Mesa Verde National Park. NPS cliff dwelling tours page (read November 2026): the 2026 tour season ran May 4 to October 21 and has ended; tickets only from recreation.gov or 1-877-444-6777, released 14 days ahead at 8 a.m. MDT and gone fast; everyone must climb the ladders unaided; bring a printed or saved ticket, as there is almost no phone signal. The park entrance fee was not checked. Where to park for the overlook and tours was not checked (OpenStreetMap\'s lot point fell on the road).',
  [W('Cliff Palace'),L('NPS: Cliff Dwelling Tours','https://www.nps.gov/meve/planyourvisit/cliff_dwelling_tours.htm')],brick='?',
  hours='Ranger-guided tours by ticket, usually daily in season (May 4 to October 21 in 2026)'),
N('mayflower-mill-silverton','Mayflower Mill (Shenandoah-Dives Mill)','Silverton','co','industry','standing',37.82903,-107.6275,'exact','open','drive','','1929–1991',
  'Built in six months in 1929 for the Shenandoah-Dives Mining Company, the mill took ore from the Mayflower Mine by an aerial tramway over 9,000 feet long and recovered gold, silver, lead, zinc and copper by gravity and froth flotation, up to 1,000 tons a day. It ran regularly until 1945; the historical society says it closed in 1991, after which Sunnyside Gold gave it to the San Juan County Historical Society. It became a National Historic Landmark in 2000.',
  'A multistorey wood-frame mill sheathed in corrugated metal, stepped down the hillside so ore fed by gravity. Wikipedia: the only intact and working mill of its kind in Colorado, with its original equipment still in order; the society\'s page adds the tram house, where the buckets and tram system are still in place. Self-guided tours inside in summer.',
  'A 1929 flotation mill with its machinery and tram buckets still in place.',
  'Owned by the San Juan County Historical Society, about 2 miles north-east of Silverton on the north side of County Road 2 (its page, read November 2026: closed for the 2026 season because of snow, reopening around Memorial Day 2027; self-guided tours in the summer months). Tour times and fees not checked. Parking at the mill not checked.',
  [W('Shenandoah-Dives Mill'),L('San Juan County Historical Society: Mayflower Mill','https://sanjuancountyhistoricalsociety.org/mayflower-mill.html')],brick='no',
  hours='Summer only, about Memorial Day until snow; self-guided tours; times not checked'),
N('baca-house-trinidad','Baca House (Trinidad History Museum)','Trinidad','co','landmark','standing',37.16915,-104.5027,'site','open','drive','','1869–1870',
  'A two-storey, ten-room adobe house built in 1869 or 1870 on Trinidad\'s Main Street for John Hough, a Santa Fe Trail merchant. Rancher and businessman Felipe Baca and his wife Dolores bought it for 22,000 pounds of wool. A long adobe outbuilding of twelve rooms behind it housed ranch hands and visitors. It was listed on the National Register in 1970 and is now part of History Colorado\'s Trinidad History Museum with the Bloom Mansion next door.',
  'The adobe house and its outbuilding, with heritage gardens; the museum block also holds the Bloom Mansion and the Santa Fe Trail Museum. All are open for self-guided tours; the upper floors of the Baca House have no wheelchair access. Original fittings inside not checked.',
  'A Santa Fe Trail adobe bought with 22,000 pounds of wool.',
  'History Colorado\'s page (read November 2026): enter through the gate on Chestnut Street; on-street parking round about; paths in the grounds are mostly brick and on a hill. Admission prices not checked.',
  [W('Baca House and Outbuilding'),L('History Colorado: Trinidad History Museum','https://historycolorado.org/trinidad-history-museum')],brick='no',
  hours='Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('miramont-castle','Miramont Castle','Manitou Springs','co','landmark','standing',38.85935,-104.92255,'exact','open','drive','','1895',
  'Father Jean Baptist Francolon, a French-born Catholic priest who had served missions in the Southwest, came to Manitou Springs for his health in 1892 and began this house in the fall of 1895, putting in every feature he liked: Wikipedia counts nine architectural styles, from Queen Anne and Romanesque to Tudor and Flemish stepped gables. The Francolons left about 1900. The Sisters of Mercy bought it in 1904 and from 1907 ran it as the Montcalm Sanitarium for some twenty years. It is in the Manitou Springs Historic District.',
  'Over 14,000 square feet, with few square rooms; the Manitou Springs Historical Society shows 42 furnished rooms and has a tea room and gardens. Interior open on the self-guided visit.',
  'A priest\'s house in nine styles that became a sanitarium.',
  'Owned and run by the Manitou Springs Historical Society at 9 Capitol Hill Avenue. Its hours page (read November 2026): adults $15, seniors $13, children $10; closed Easter, Thanksgiving, Christmas Eve, Christmas Day and New Year\'s Eve; tea is extra. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Miramont Castle'),L('Miramont Castle: hours and fees','https://miramontcastle.org/castle-hours')],brick='?',
  hours='Tuesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. Memorial Day to Labor Day; last entry 30 minutes before closing'),
N('rosemount-pueblo','Rosemount','Pueblo','co','landmark','standing',38.28027,-104.61273,'exact','open','drive','','1891–1893',
  'A 37-room mansion begun in 1891 and finished in 1893 for John A. Thatcher, who came from Pennsylvania and made his money in dry goods, banking, mining and cattle, and his wife Margaret, after whose favourite flower it was named. Architect Henry Hudson Holly designed it, and it was built of pink rhyolite. The family lived there until Raymond Thatcher died in 1968; it then went to the city and became a museum. It was listed on the National Register in 1974.',
  'The house museum says nearly all the furnishings, paintings, custom woodwork and wall and window treatments are original to the home; Wikipedia names cherry, mahogany, maple and oak inside. A carriage house stands on the grounds. Seen by guided tour.',
  'A Pueblo banker\'s 37 rooms, still holding nearly all their own furniture.',
  'Run as a non-profit museum at 419 West 14th Street. Its own page (read November 2026): admission from $4, members free; closed in January for cleaning, reopening in February; Christmas at Rosemount in November and December. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Rosemount Museum'),L('Rosemount Museum','https://rosemount.org/')],brick='no',
  hours='Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 2:30 p.m., tours on the half hour; closed in January'),
N('alpine-tunnel-station','Alpine Tunnel station','Pitkin','co','rail','ruins',38.6405,-106.4079,'site','public','drive','','1880–1910',
  'The Denver, South Park and Pacific bored the Alpine Tunnel under Altman (later Alpine) Pass in 1880 and 1881, the first railroad tunnel under the Continental Divide in Colorado and, at 11,523 feet, the highest in North America when it opened in 1882. Loose rock and clay meant heavy timbering and 18 months of work. The Colorado and Southern gave the line up in 1910 after damage inside the tunnel that was not worth mending for so little traffic.',
  'Both portals are closed: the east one collapsed and the west one is buried by slides. At the west end, Wikipedia says, volunteers have restored the 1883 telegraph office and rebuilt the platform, 120 feet of track, a turntable and an outhouse; the section house and engine house are ruins. The old grade is a trail on the east side and a rough road on the west.',
  'The highest railroad tunnel in North America in 1882, given up in 1910.',
  'Gunnison National Forest, Alpine Tunnel Historic District. The Forest Service page (read November 2026): day use only, sunrise to sunset, no fee; from Pitkin take Forest Road 765 (Cumberland Pass Road) 3 miles, then Forest Road 839 for 10 miles to the station complex, a narrow road on the old grade where drivers must take care. Closure orders for nearby roads are listed on the page; the road\'s season was not checked. Pin on the buildings OpenStreetMap maps at the station.',
  [W('Alpine Tunnel'),L('Forest Service: Alpine Tunnel Historic District','https://www.fs.usda.gov/r02/gmug/recreation/alpine-tunnel-historic-district')],brick='?',
  hours='Day use, sunrise to sunset'),
N('hahns-peak-schoolhouse','Hahns Peak Schoolhouse','Hahns Peak','co','civic','standing',40.80627,-106.9444,'site','open','drive','','1911–1943',
  'Hahns Peak Village was the county seat for north-western Colorado from 1877 to 1912. Its one-room school, built in 1911, taught all grades until it closed in the 1940s (Wikipedia gives 1943, SAH Archipedia 1942) and then served as the place for meetings, dances, church services and funerals. The Hahns Peak Area Historical Society has owned and run it as a museum since 1973; it was listed on the National Register in 1974.',
  'A frame schoolhouse with an open bell tower, known as the little green school house; SAH Archipedia says the coal shed and two 1911 privies survive and the horse barn is gone. The society\'s museum of 1980 next door keeps the town\'s steel-barred "bear trap" jail. Open in summer.',
  'A one-room school in the old county seat of north-western Colorado.',
  'Hahns Peak Area Historical Society, on Main Street. Its page (read November 2026): from U.S. 40 take County Road 129 (Elk River Road) north 24 miles; the schoolhouse and museum are on the right; free. Pin is Wikipedia\'s coordinate; parking not checked.',
  [W('Hahns Peak Schoolhouse'),L('Hahns Peak Area Historical Society','https://hahnspeakhistoric.com/'),L('SAH Archipedia: Hahns Peak Schoolhouse','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/9548')],conf='mixed',brick='no',
  hours='Daily 12 to 4 p.m., Memorial Day to Labor Day, weather permitting'),
N('lodore-school-browns-park','Lodore School','Maybell','co','civic','standing',40.78006,-108.89287,'exact','view','drive','','1911–1947',
  'Carpenters Evers, Hunt and Hoover built this school in 1911 for the ranch children of Browns Park, the remote valley Wikipedia calls a hideout for Butch Cassidy and others. It taught them until 1947. The Browns Park Women\'s Club took it over in 1954 as a community centre, and it stayed one after the state wildlife agency took control in 1970. It was listed on the National Register in 1975 (as the Old Ladore School; the Register form says Lodore is the right spelling).',
  'A one-storey building of horizontal planks with a cupola bell tower that hides a brick chimney. Interior not checked.',
  'The one-room school of the outlaw valley.',
  'By the Green River on State Highway 318, about 85 miles west of Craig and 5 miles from the Gates of Lodore. Who owns it now and whether it opens were not checked: look from the road.',
  [W('Old Ladore School')],brick='?'),
]
